============================================================
MView Development Log
Day 5
============================================================

Status

Bootstrap phase completed.
The individual subsystems now collaborate to form the application's
first complete processing pipeline.

------------------------------------------------------------
Major Milestone
------------------------------------------------------------

Current application flow:

    TMainForm
        │
        ▼
    TMView
        │
        ▼
    TMediaLoader
        │
        ▼
    TMedia
        │
        ▼
    TRenderer

Although nothing is rendered yet, this establishes the complete
application workflow.

------------------------------------------------------------
New Unit
------------------------------------------------------------

Created:

    uRenderer

Responsibilities

- Display media.
- Own rendering resources.
- Apply view transformations.

Does NOT

- Load media.
- Navigate directories.
- Handle mouse input.

------------------------------------------------------------
Architecture
------------------------------------------------------------

Established the distinction between

Coordinator

    TMView

and Workers

    TConfig
    TNavigator
    TMediaLoader
    TRenderer

TMView coordinates the application.

Subsystems perform one clearly defined task.

------------------------------------------------------------
TMView.OpenMedia()
------------------------------------------------------------

Added the first real application command.

Responsibilities

- Request media from TMediaLoader.
- Receive a TMedia object.
- Pass the media to TRenderer.

This is the first successful collaboration between independent
subsystems.

------------------------------------------------------------
Ownership
------------------------------------------------------------

Adopted the ownership rule:

"The object that creates another object is responsible for
destroying it."

Current ownership tree

    TMainForm
        │
        ▼
    TMView
        │
        ├── TConfig
        ├── TNavigator
        ├── TMediaLoader
        └── TRenderer

Future classes will explicitly document ownership.

Example

    { Owned subsystems
      Created in Create.
      Destroyed in Destroy.
    }

------------------------------------------------------------
Object-Oriented Concept
------------------------------------------------------------

Important conceptual breakthrough.

Difference between

Local variables

- temporary
- exist only during a procedure call

Fields

- belong to the object
- exist during the object's lifetime
- represent what the object knows

Example

TConfig stores

    FLastDirectory
    FWrapAround
    FSortMode
    FCacheSizeMB

These values remain available throughout the application's
entire lifetime.

This marks an important transition from procedural programming
toward object-oriented thinking.

------------------------------------------------------------
Configuration
------------------------------------------------------------

Designed Version 1 of MView.ini.

Sections

    Startup
    Window
    View
    Navigation
    Performance
    Mouse
    Renderer

Design decisions

- Command line overrides configuration.
- Configuration stores user preferences.
- Session information will eventually be stored separately.
- TConfig exposes typed properties.
- Only TConfig reads and writes the INI file.

------------------------------------------------------------
TConfig
------------------------------------------------------------

Designed the first real interface.

Responsibilities

- Read MView.ini
- Validate configuration
- Store configuration as object state
- Save configuration

Configuration values are stored as object fields,
not temporary variables.

------------------------------------------------------------
Hamana Discussion
------------------------------------------------------------

Analyzed Hamana's interaction model.

Observation

The image itself becomes the manipulated object rather than
being constrained inside a window.

Future renderer model

    Media

        +

    ViewState

        ↓

    Renderer

        ↓

    Screen

Only the ViewState changes.

The original media remains unchanged.

------------------------------------------------------------
Future ViewState
------------------------------------------------------------

Identified a future class.

    TViewState

Expected responsibilities

- Zoom
- Rotation
- Pan
- Mirror
- Fit mode
- Interpolation

Renderer will transform the media according to the ViewState.

------------------------------------------------------------
Mouse Architecture
------------------------------------------------------------

Decided against a transparent overlay window.

Instead

    Windows Messages

            ↓

      Mouse Engine

            ↓

        Commands

            ↓

         TMView

Mouse events become commands.

Subsystems never receive raw Windows mouse messages.

This architecture naturally supports future scripting.

------------------------------------------------------------
Coding Standard
------------------------------------------------------------

Added project documentation standard.

Each important class should document

- Purpose
- Owns
- Knows
- Responsibilities
- Does NOT

These comments describe architecture rather than implementation.

------------------------------------------------------------
Lazarus Lesson
------------------------------------------------------------

Encountered a corrupted form-designer state
("dangling reference").

Resolution

Recreated the form.

Because application logic already resides outside the form,
the recovery was straightforward.

Lesson

Forms should remain thin shells.

